### What is multi-catch?

Multi-catch (Java 7) lets a single catch block handle several exception types joined with the pipe symbol (`|`). It was introduced to remove the duplicate code you get when two or more separate catch blocks have identical bodies.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.io.IOException;
import java.sql.SQLException;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class MultiCatchBasicDemo {

    // BEFORE: separate catches with identical bodies
    public static void separateCatches(String accountId) {
        try {
            loadFromFile(accountId);
            loadFromDatabase(accountId);
        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.out.println("Export failed for " + accountId + ": " + e.getMessage());
        } catch (SQLException e) {
            System.out.println("Export failed for " + accountId + ": " + e.getMessage());
            // Identical body. Duplication risk.
        }
    }

    // AFTER: multi-catch eliminates duplication
    public static void multiCatch(String accountId) {
        try {
            loadFromFile(accountId);
            loadFromDatabase(accountId);
        } catch (IOException | SQLException e) {
            System.out.println("Export failed for " + accountId + ": " + e.getMessage());
            // One block, one body. No duplication.
        }
    }

    private static void loadFromFile(String id) throws IOException {
        throw new IOException("File not found: " + id + ".csv");
    }

    private static void loadFromDatabase(String id) throws SQLException {
        throw new SQLException("Connection refused for " + id);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        multiCatch("SAV2001");
    }
}
```

```text
Export failed for SAV2001: File not found: SAV2001.csv
```

### How does multi-catch differ from separate catch blocks?

| Feature | Separate catches | Multi-catch |
|---|---|---|
| Syntax | One block per type | `catch (A \| B e)` |
| Variable finality | `e` is not final | `e` is implicitly final |
| Code duplication | Bodies can be duplicated | Single body, no duplication |
| Reassignment | `e = new X()` is allowed | `e = new X()` is compile error |
| Bytecode | Separate handlers | One handler, multiple table entries |
| Types | Any type per block | Must be unrelated (no parent-child) |

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.io.IOException;
import java.sql.SQLException;

public class MultiVsSeparateDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Multi-catch: e is implicitly final
        try {
            throw new IOException("test");
        } catch (IOException | SQLException e) {
            // e = new IOException("replaced");  // COMPILE ERROR: e is final
            System.out.println("Multi-catch, final e: " + e.getMessage());
        }

        // Single catch: e is NOT final
        try {
            throw new IOException("original");
        } catch (IOException e) {
            e = new IOException("replaced");  // Allowed
            System.out.println("Single catch, reassigned e: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Multi-catch, final e: test
Single catch, reassigned e: replaced
```

### Why must multi-catch types be unrelated?

The compiler prevents parent-child pairs because the parent already covers the child. Including both would be redundant.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.io.FileNotFoundException;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.sql.SQLException;

public class MultiCatchRelationshipDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // VALID: IOException and SQLException are unrelated siblings
        try {
            throw new IOException("IO error");
        } catch (IOException | SQLException e) {
            System.out.println("Valid: " + e.getClass().getSimpleName());
        }

        // INVALID: FileNotFoundException IS-A IOException (parent-child)
        // try { throw new IOException("x"); }
        // catch (IOException | FileNotFoundException e) { }  // COMPILE ERROR!
        // Error: "Alternatives in a multi-catch statement cannot be related by subclassing"

        // Three or more types are valid:
        try {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("bad input");
        } catch (IOException | SQLException | IllegalArgumentException e) {
            System.out.println("Three types: " + e.getClass().getSimpleName());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Valid: IOException
Three types: IllegalArgumentException
```

### When should you use multi-catch vs separate catches?

Use multi-catch when the handling logic is identical for multiple exception types. Use separate catches when each exception type needs different handling.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.io.IOException;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.sql.SQLException;

public class WhenToUseMultiCatch {

    // USE multi-catch: same handling for both
    public static void sameHandling(String accountId) {
        try {
            loadData(accountId);
        } catch (IOException | SQLException e) {
            System.out.println("Data load failed for " + accountId + ": " + e.getMessage());
            // Both get the same logging and fallback logic
        }
    }

    // USE separate catches: different handling per type
    public static void differentHandling(String accountId) {
        try {
            loadData(accountId);
        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.out.println("IO: trying fallback file for " + accountId);
            // Fallback to backup file
        } catch (SQLException e) {
            System.out.println("SQL: retrying database connection for " + accountId);
            // Retry with different connection
        }
    }

    private static void loadData(String id) throws IOException, SQLException {
        throw new IOException("File not found");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        sameHandling("SAV2001");
        differentHandling("CUR3001");
    }
}
```

```text
Data load failed for SAV2001: File not found
IO: trying fallback file for CUR3001
```

### What are common mistakes with multi-catch?

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.io.IOException;
import java.sql.SQLException;

public class MultiCatchMistakesDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // WRONG: trying to reassign multi-catch variable
        // try { throw new IOException("x"); }
        // catch (IOException | SQLException e) {
        //     e = new IOException("replaced");  // COMPILE ERROR: e is final
        // }

        // WRONG: parent-child in multi-catch
        // catch (Exception | IOException e) { }  // COMPILE ERROR

        // CORRECT: use multi-catch for unrelated types
        try {
            throw new IOException("trade file missing");
        } catch (IOException | SQLException e) {
            System.out.println("Correct multi-catch: " + e.getMessage());
        }

        // CORRECT: if you need to reassign, use separate catch
        try {
            throw new IOException("original");
        } catch (IOException e) {
            e = new IOException("wrapped: " + e.getMessage());
            System.out.println("Reassigned in single catch: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Correct multi-catch: trade file missing
Reassigned in single catch: wrapped: original
```

### What about Java 7 precise rethrow with multi-catch?

When you catch a broad type and rethrow without reassigning, the compiler narrows the type based on what the try block actually throws. This works with both single catch and multi-catch.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.io.IOException;
import java.sql.SQLException;

public class PreciseRethrowDemo {

    // Java 7+: compiler knows only IOException or SQLException can reach here
    public static void process() throws IOException, SQLException {
        try {
            readFile();   // throws IOException
            queryDb();    // throws SQLException
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println("Logging: " + e.getMessage());
            throw e;  // Compiler allows throws IOException, SQLException
            // NOT throws Exception (because e was not reassigned)
        }
    }

    private static void readFile() throws IOException {
        throw new IOException("File error");
    }

    private static void queryDb() throws SQLException {
        throw new SQLException("DB error");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            process();
        } catch (IOException | SQLException e) {
            System.out.println("Caught: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Logging: File error
Caught: File error
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Bytecode optimization.** Multi-catch compiles to one handler entry that several exception-table rows point to. This avoids duplicating bytecode for identical handler bodies.
- **Type inference.** In multi-catch, the type of `e` is the common supertype of all listed types. For `catch (IOException | SQLException e)`, the type is `Exception` (their common parent), but the compiler restricts reassignment.
- **Maintenance benefit.** The biggest advantage of multi-catch is not performance but maintenance. With separate catches, identical bodies can drift apart during edits. Multi-catch has one body to maintain.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Can multi-catch have more than two types? (Yes: `catch (A | B | C e)`.)
- Why is the variable final in multi-catch? (Compiler cannot know the concrete type at compile time.)
- Can you use multi-catch with checked and unchecked together? (Yes: `catch (IOException | IllegalArgumentException e)`.)
- How does multi-catch affect the method's throws clause? (It does not change it. The method must still declare any checked types.)
